# The memory guard. A GB10's GPU and CPU share one memory, and nothing watched what the engine left free: on 2026-10-12
# node2 fell to 471 MB available while serving, from torch's cache of freed GPU blocks (+2.2 GiB in two hours; Python's
# heap grew 0.34 GiB in 28 hours). Each rank reads its own MemAvailable about once a second on its engine loop: normal
# from 2.0 GiB, and back to normal only above it; tight below 1.5 GiB: torch's unused cache released and a full Python
# collection, at most every 30 s; critical below 1.0 GiB: also every saved GPU state dropped on both ranks (the spill
# tier wrote them when they were kept: they come back on their next turn), at most every 5 s. Admission never stops.
# While streams are live, rank 0 sends MEMQ about once a second and both ranks all-gather four numbers (level,
# MemAvailable, torch reserved, torch allocated): /health's memory_guard shows both Sparks, and a critical node2 makes
# rank 0 drop the states. TF_GLM_MEMORY_GUARD: "0" off, else "normal,tight,critical" in GiB (default 2.0,1.5,1.0).
# memory_guard.py never changes a stored state (spill.NEUTRAL). mgllm, 2026-10-13.
# Tests: tests/test_9024_memory_guard.py (CPU).

